\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

We evaluate vision token pruning across six VLA architectures spanning three connector families on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}, which provides four task suites of increasing complexity: Object, Spatial, Goal, and Long (10 tasks $\times$ 50 episodes each).

Four models are evaluated in closed-loop control: $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} (4B, linear projection, 768 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.055), X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M, linear + 12-layer BART encoder, 50 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.151), OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.215), and SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M, pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$, 128 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.314). Two additional models ($\pi_{0}$~\cite{black2024pi0}, OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft}) are analyzed via offline metrics only. Table~\ref{tab:model_properties} summarizes all six architectures. We prune at $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$, reporting mean success rate (SR) over 500 episodes.

\subsection{Two-Axis Framework Overview}
\label{sec:two_axis}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_pruning_overview.pdf}
\caption{Pruning degradation on LIBERO-Object (L2 norm scoring). $\pi_{0.5}$ maintains ${\geq}$98\% through $k{=}0.85$, OpenVLA degrades linearly, X-VLA cliffs at $k{=}0.7$, SmolVLA collapses to 0\% at $k{=}0.95$.}
\label{fig:pruning_overview}
\end{figure}

Three qualitatively distinct response patterns emerge across the connector spectrum (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}; Figure~\ref{fig:pruning_overview}); a fourth (OpenVLA, MLP connector) is detailed in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:full_results}.

\textbf{$\pi_{0.5}$ (linear, CV$\,{=}\,$0.055).} Low-CV models are \emph{uniformly critical}: which tokens are removed matters more than how many. L2 maintains ${\geq}$98.0\% through $k{=}0.85$, while random at $k{=}0.9$ collapses to 9.0\%---an 89\,pp method gap from identical removal fractions.

\textbf{X-VLA (BART encoder, CV$\,{=}\,$0.151).} The BART encoder distributes information uniformly, making the model method-insensitive but task-sensitive. At $k{=}0.9$ on Object, L2 and random differ by 0.4\,pp---yet the same model drops 27.4\,pp on Goal. On Long, removing 3 of 50 tokens ($k{=}0.95$) halves SR regardless of scoring method.

\textbf{OpenVLA (MLP, CV$\,{=}\,$0.215).} OpenVLA exhibits scoring crossover: L2 outperforms random on Object but the ranking inverts on Spatial and Goal at $k{=}0.9$ (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:full_results}; Section~\ref{sec:analysis}).

\textbf{SmolVLA (pixel-shuffle, CV$\,{=}\,$0.314).} Removing 6 of 128 tokens ($k{=}0.95$) collapses SR to ${\leq}$6.6\% across all suites and methods---a catastrophic cliff detailed below.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Closed-loop success rates (\%) on LIBERO under L2 norm (L2) and random (R) token pruning. SmolVLA collapses to ${\leq}$6.6\% across all conditions. $\pi_{0.5}$ Long: five-seed mean ($n{=}5$; per-seed in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:pi05_long_multiseed}). Full results including OpenVLA in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:full_results}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
