\subsection{Pixel-Shuffle Entanglement}
\label{sec:pixel_shuffle}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_pixel_shuffle.pdf}
\caption{Pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding: each 16-patch block is channel-concatenated into one token. Removing any token eliminates a non-overlapping receptive field with no redundancy.}
\label{fig:pixel_shuffle}
\end{figure}

SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding concatenates 16 patches per token (Figure~\ref{fig:pixel_shuffle}), creating non-overlapping receptive fields. This collapses SR to ${\leq}$6.6\% at $k{=}0.95$ across all suites and methods, verified on two checkpoints (44.8\% and 59.2\% baselines). No scoring function can identify dispensable tokens because all carry spatially entangled information; token-independent connectors retain per-patch locality, so partial removal preserves spatial coverage.

\subsection{Position Disruption vs.\ Information Loss}
\label{sec:zeromask}

$\pi_{0.5}$'s random pruning at $k{=}0.9$ collapses to 9.0\% on Object despite removing only 10\% of tokens. We isolate the mechanism by comparing hard pruning (physically removes tokens, re-indexes positions) with zero-masking (replaces token embeddings with zeros, preserves positions). Both destroy identical visual information; only hard pruning alters positional structure. Zero-masking preserves 98.0\% SR ($-$0.2\,pp from baseline), while hard pruning collapses to 9.0\%. The 89\,pp gap is entirely attributable to position disruption: $\pi_{0.5}$'s linear connector with learned positional encodings tolerates complete signal destruction but not index shifts.

\subsection{Scoring Method Sensitivity and CV Diagnostic}
\label{sec:cv_sensitivity}

CV predicts where scoring method choice becomes critical (Table~\ref{tab:model_properties}; Figure~\ref{fig:method_gap}). Low-CV models are maximally method-sensitive: $\pi_{0.5}$'s Object gap at $k{=}0.95$ reaches 24.2\,pp, exceeding X-VLA's maximum (1.4\,pp) by $17{\times}$. As CV increases, sensitivity decreases but does not vanish---OpenVLA's method gap at $k{=}0.9$ ranges from $+$7.8\,pp to $-$4.8\,pp across suites (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:full_results}). At $k{=}0.5$, method-independence breaks down: $\Delta$(R$-$L2) on Object is $+$36.0\,pp ($\pi_{0.5}$), $+$27.2\,pp (X-VLA), confirming that random preserves spatial diversity while L2 clusters correlated tokens.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_method_gap.pdf}
\caption{Method gap $|\text{SR(L2)} - \text{SR(Random)}|$ at $k{=}0.95$. $\pi_{0.5}$'s Object gap (24.2\,pp) exceeds X-VLA's maximum (1.4\,pp) by $17{\times}$.}
\label{fig:method_gap}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Architecture-dependent scoring.} The optimal scoring method depends on the connector (Table~\ref{tab:attention_pruning}). On X-VLA at $k{=}0.5$, attention-based scoring recovers near-baseline performance (${\geq}$92.8\%) while L2 falls $8$--$27$\,pp below random---because the BART encoder's cross-token attention renders L2 norms anti-informative. On $\pi_{0.5}$, attention is catastrophically harmful on Object ($-$30.4\,pp) because attention scores select semantically relevant tokens that lack the spatial precision this architecture depends on. On Long, attention degrades steeply to a plateau ($-$26.5\,pp from $k{=}0.95$ to $k{=}0.85$, then flat through $k{=}0.5$; Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}).

\textbf{Disruption peak.} Random pruning on $\pi_{0.5}$ produces a counterintuitive failure: intermediate removal is \emph{more} destructive than aggressive removal. On Long at $k{=}0.9$, the margin peaks at 46.9\,pp (L2 80.4\% vs.\ Random 33.6\%; Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}). The margin follows an asymmetric inverted-U: $k{=}0.95$ (25.6\,pp) to crossing zero near $k{\approx}0.71$; at $k{=}0.5$, all methods converge within ${\sim}$2\,pp (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:margin_profile}). On Object at $k{=}0.85$, random collapses to 3.6--5.6\% while L2 maintains 99.0\% ($+$90\,pp margin). The V-shaped random recovery arises from position aliasing under scattered removal (Section~\ref{sec:analysis}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_cliff_highlight.pdf}
\caption{Scoring method determines cliff behavior on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long (five-seed mean $\pm$ s.d.\ shaded; $n{=}5$). L2 degrades monotonically; attention exhibits a sharp cliff from $k{=}0.95$ to $k{=}0.85$ ($-$26.5\,pp), then plateaus; random exhibits a V-shape with collapse at $k{=}0.9$ (46.9\,pp gap vs.\ L2) and recovery at $k{\leq}0.7$.}
\label{fig:cliff_highlight}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Importance margin gradient.} L2's advantage shrinks monotonically with task complexity on $\pi_{0.5}$ at $k{=}0.85$: Object $+$93\,pp $>$ Goal $+$35.2\,pp $>$ Long $+$33.2\,pp. Multi-step tasks distribute critical information across tokens with heterogeneous norm profiles, making L2 norms a progressively weaker proxy for token utility.

\subsection{VTR Recovery}
\label{sec:vtr_recovery}

Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) addresses the catastrophic cliff through learned re-encoding rather than hard deletion. A cross-attention module (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA) uses $M$ learned queries to attend over all 128 source tokens, recovering SmolVLA from 0.0\% to over 60\% SR at 75\% compression ($M{=}32$: 60.1 $\pm$ 1.3\%, 3 seeds). A parameter-matched MLP adapter achieves comparable performance (63.4 $\pm$ 2.6\%, $p{=}0.15$), confirming the gain stems from re-encoding itself, not cross-attention specifically. Cross-suite generalization: Spatial 53.2\%, Goal 56.4\%, both from 0.0\%.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{VTR re-encoding on SmolVLA (LIBERO-Object). Hard pruning collapses all configurations to 0\%; VTR recovers ${\geq}$57\% at up to 87.5\% compression.}
\label{tab:vtr_main}
\begin{tabular}{lccr}
\toprule
Configuration & $M$ & Compress.\ & SR (\%) \\
\midrule
\textit{Baseline (unpruned)} & 128 & 0\% & 44.8 \\
\textit{Hard pruning (any)} & -- & 5--50\% & 0.0 \\
\midrule
Cross-attn VTR & 32 & 75\% & 60.1 $\pm$ 1.3 \\
