\subsection{Pruning Protocol}
\label{sec:pruning_protocol}

We apply post-connector token pruning at retention ratio $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$, retaining $\lfloor k \cdot N \rfloor$ tokens. Two strategies bracket expected performance: \textbf{L2 norm selection} retains the top-$k$ fraction by L2 norm, and \textbf{random subsampling} selects $\lfloor k \cdot N \rfloor$ tokens uniformly at random. The \emph{importance margin} at ratio $k$ is $\text{SR}_{\text{L2}}(k) - \text{SR}_{\text{Random}}(k)$; a positive margin indicates token importance structure is informative. Both strategies physically remove unselected tokens and re-index position IDs. We prune post-connector to isolate the connector's effect on token information structure.

\subsection{Compressibility Diagnostic: Importance CV}
\label{sec:cv_diagnostic}

We quantify post-connector token heterogeneity via the coefficient of variation (CV) of L2 norms:
\begin{equation}
\text{CV} = \frac{\sigma\!\left(\{\|t_i\|_2\}_{i=1}^{N}\right)}{\mu\!\left(\{\|t_i\|_2\}_{i=1}^{N}\right)}
\label{eq:cv}
\end{equation}
CV is a single-pass diagnostic requiring one forward pass with no downstream evaluation. It captures a continuous spectrum from $\pi_{0}$ (CV$\,{=}\,$0.038, near-uniform norms) to SmolVLA (CV$\,{=}\,$0.314, high heterogeneity). Low-CV models have near-uniform tokens where \emph{which} tokens are removed matters more than \emph{how many}; high-CV entangling connectors fail catastrophically under any removal strategy.

\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}
\label{sec:vtr}

Hard pruning fails for token-entangling connectors because removing any token destroys spatially distributed information. VTR addresses this through learned re-encoding: a cross-attention module (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA) uses $M$ learned queries to attend over all $N$ source tokens. The compression ratio is controlled by $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$; $M{=}N$ serves as a passthrough control.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig_hero_overview.pdf}
\caption{Overview of VLA token compressibility.
\textbf{(a)}~The same robot observation produces qualitatively different token importance patterns through different vision connectors, quantified by the coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token norms.
\textbf{(b)}~Connector architecture determines pruning tolerance: independent connectors degrade gracefully, entangling connectors exhibit cliffs; task complexity amplifies the divergence.
\textbf{(c)}~A single forward pass CV diagnostic routes each architecture to an appropriate compression strategy.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure*}

Remove 5\% of vision tokens from SmolVLA and its success rate collapses from 59.2\% to near-zero (${\leq}$6.6\%). Apply the same reduction to $\pi_{0.5}$ and it retains 98.2\% under L2 norm selection. Both are Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on a ViT encoder feeding tokens through a connector into a language model backbone; the critical difference is the connector architecture. Token pruning is well-established for ViTs~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit,bolya2023tome,liang2022evit} and extends to VLAs~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap,xu2025vlacache,yang2025efficientvla,liu2025vlapruner}, but each method validates on a single architecture, implicitly assuming pruning transfers across designs.

Yet current VLAs employ fundamentally different vision connectors---linear projections~\cite{black2024pi0}, MLP projectors~\cite{kim2024openvla}, pixel-shuffle folding~\cite{shukor2025smolvla}, and learned encoders with cross-token self-attention~\cite{zheng2025xvla}---each imposing distinct constraints on how visual information distributes across tokens. Deploying a pruning method validated on one connector type to another risks silent degradation or complete failure.

We present an empirical study of VLA token compressibility, identifying the vision-language connector as the primary determinant of pruning robustness (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Token-independent connectors degrade gracefully; entangling connectors produce catastrophic failures amplified by task complexity ($20{\times}$ from Object to Goal). The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms captures this on a continuous spectrum (0.038 to 0.314). Under sufficient compression, scoring effectiveness becomes connector-dependent: L2 norm aids $\pi_{0.5}$ (up to $+$46.9\,pp on Long at $k{=}0.9$; $+$35.2\,pp on Goal at $k{=}0.85$) but reverses at aggressive ratios ($-$36\,pp at $k{=}0.5$); on X-VLA it is anti-informative ($-$8 to $-$27\,pp), while attention recovers near-baseline performance. For pixel-shuffle connectors where all pruning fails, Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR, ${\sim}$5\% extra parameters) recovers over 60\% SR at 75\% compression.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item A compressibility analysis across five VLA architectures (450M--7B) on four LIBERO suites, identifying connector architecture as the primary determinant of pruning robustness.
    \item CV of token L2 norms as a single-pass diagnostic for connector compressibility type (uniform, graded, catastrophic), validated on four closed-loop architectures.
    \item A zero-mask ablation proving position disruption---not information loss---drives $\pi_{0.5}$'s 89\,pp failure gap. VTR recovers a pixel-shuffle VLA from 0\% to over 60\% at 75\% compression.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{ViT token pruning.}
DynamicViT~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit}, EViT~\cite{liang2022evit}, A-ViT~\cite{yin2022avit}, and Token Merging~\cite{bolya2023tome} remove 50--66\% of tokens with under 0.5\,pp ImageNet accuracy loss, but robotic manipulation inverts the spatial-insensitivity assumption. None has been evaluated in closed-loop VLA control.

\paragraph{Token efficiency in VLMs and VLAs.}
FastV~\cite{chen2024fastv} and PruMerge~\cite{shang2024prumerge} reduce vision tokens in VLMs using attention signals. VLA-specific methods have emerged: VLA-IAP~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap}, VLA-Cache~\cite{xu2025vlacache}, EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla}, VLA-Pruner~\cite{liu2025vlapruner}, LightVLA~\cite{jiang2026lightvla}, and Compressor-VLA~\cite{gao2025compressorvla}. Each validates on a single architecture; we ask how architectural choices determine which compression approaches succeed or fail.

\paragraph{Vision connectors.}
LLaVA~\cite{liu2023llava} projects patches independently through an MLP; BLIP-2~\cite{li2023blip2} uses cross-attention queries; pixel shuffle~\cite{shi2016subpixel} folds spatial neighborhoods into channels. These create qualitatively different token dependencies whose pruning consequences have not been characterized. We provide the first cross-architecture analysis of how connector coupling and task complexity jointly govern VLA token compressibility.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Connector architecture is the primary factor governing VLA token compressibility. Across five architectures and four LIBERO suites, token-independent connectors degrade gracefully; entangling connectors exhibit catastrophic cliffs amplified by task complexity ($20{\times}$ from Object to Goal). CV partitions models into uniform, graded, and catastrophic classes in a single forward pass. Under sufficient compression, scoring effectiveness becomes connector- and k-dependent: at $k{=}0.9$ on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long, L2 preserves 80\% while random collapses to 34\% (46.9\,pp gap). A zero-mask ablation confirms position disruption---not information loss---drives the failure. Where all deletion fails, VTR recovers over 60\% SR at 75\% compression. Real-robot validation and adaptive per-task pruning schedules remain open. \textbf{AI disclosure:} This work used AI tools (Claude) for code development and manuscript drafting; all scientific claims are the authors' own.

